  "facts": [
    "The Mackinac Bridge, opened November 1, 1957, stretches 26,372 feet, just shy of five miles, making it the longest suspension bridge between anchorages in the Western Hemisphere and a true engineering marvel.",
    "Designed by engineer David B. Steinman, the bridge’s 3,800‑foot main span ranks as the third‑longest suspension span in the United States, a record achieved after decades of planning that began in the 1880s.",
    "Before the bridge, ferries ferried up to 9,000 vehicles daily across the Straits, yet traffic backups could reach 16 miles; the bridge eliminated those delays and unified Michigan’s Upper and Lower Peninsulas."
